    "q-131196": {
      "dom_id": "step-q131196",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14129167/assets/4bf8de7e8719545b3d8abf5dc9fb4bc0c5f71034dba7186b7d9822d9e8be044d.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\nI. $∇\\cdot F=0$\n\nII. $∇\\cdot F>0$\n\nIII. $∇\\cdot F<0$\n\nIV. $F$ is solenoidal",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14129167/assets/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement III is true, whereas statements I and II are false. Notice that the lengths of the vector's $x$-components decrease as we move horizontally towards the origin. Therefore, the vector field $F$ acts like a sink at every point.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $∇\\cdot F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
